Q)How PUT is considered by Spring or REST, so that it know that it has to perform update operation?
Spring does not automatically know that a database update must happen just because the HTTP method is PUT. Spring identifies the HTTP method and routes the request to the 
appropriate controller method. Your application code, usually the service and repository layers, determines whether to insert, update, or replace data.
@PutMapping only maps the HTTP request to a Java method. It does not execute an SQL UPDATE statement automatically.
2. How does the actual database UPDATE happen?
That depends on how you implement your service and repository.
Approach A: Spring Data JPA — fetch, modify, and save

@Service
public class EmployeeService {

    private final EmployeeRepository repository;

    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void update(Long id, EmployeeRequest request) {

        Employee employee = repository.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() ->
                        new RuntimeException("Employee not found"));

        employee.setName(request.name());
        employee.setDepartment(request.department());

        repository.save(employee);
    }
}

The important JPA concept: Dirty checking
In this example, findById() loads the employee inside a transaction. The returned entity is managed by the JPA persistence context.
When you modify its fields, Hibernate tracks those changes. At flush time, it detects the modifications and generates the required SQL.
In this particular example, repository.save(employee) is not strictly necessary. Because the entity is already managed inside the transaction, dirty checking is sufficient to persist the changes.
The exact SQL generated depends on your entity mapping and Hibernate configuration.

